The NanoElectronic MOdeling (NEMO) Math Library (NML) is available
as a compressed tape archive nml.tgz
Copy nml.tgz into your home directory and type

	gunzip nml.tgz
	tar xvf nml.tar
	cd nml/src
	make

to uncompress and unarchive the nml/ distribution directory
then make the NML class libraries in the nml/src/ directory.
Type

	cd ../examples
	make example
	./example

to make and run the example program in the nml/examples/ directory.

The NML supports both real and complex double precision floating-point
as well as integer scalar, vector and matrix classes.

	integer		real		complex
----------------------------------------------------
scalar	nml_iscalar	nml_dscalar	nml_dcscalar
vector	nml_ivector	nml_dvector	nml_dcvector
matrix	nml_imatrix	nml_dmatrix	nml_dcmatrix

A vector is an array of scalars and
a matrix is an array of pointers to scalars
each of which points to the first element
of the corresponding row of the matrix.
The rows of the matrix are all stored together
end-to-end in a vector of scalars.

The NML defines a few types

	typedef	signed int	ptrdiff_t;
	typedef    size_t	nml_offset;
	typedef    size_t	nml_extent;
	typedef ptrdiff_t	nml_stride;

which are used as parameters:

	const nml_extent	m = 7;	/* extent of a column	*/
	const nml_extent	n = 4;	/* extent of a row	*/

in function calls.
Storage for vector and matrix objects is allocated dynamically at run time.
NML functions reference vector and matrix objects through pointers:

	nml_ivector*	pv;	/* pointer to an integer vector	*/
	nml_dvector*	pv;	/* pointer to a real vector	*/
	nml_dcvector*	pv;	/* pointer to a complex vector	*/
	nml_imatrix*	pM;	/* pointer to an integer matrix	*/
	nml_dmatrix*	pM;	/* pointer to a real matrix	*/
	nml_dcmatrix*	pM;	/* pointer to a complex matrix	*/

There are functions:

extent	integer			real			complex
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
vector	nml_iv_extent(pv)	nml_dv_extent(pv)	nml_dcv_extent(pv)
column	nml_im_extent1(pM)	nml_dm_extent1(pM)	nml_dcm_extent1(pM)
row	nml_im_extent2(pM)	nml_dm_extent2(pM)	nml_dcm_extent2(pM)

to retrieve the extent of a vector or
the extent of a column or row of a matrix.

There are functions:

vector	pseudo-constructor	destructor		reconstructor
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
integer	pv = nml_iv_new(n)	nml_iv_delete(pv)	nml_iv_resize(&pv, n)
real	pv = nml_dv_new(n)	nml_dv_delete(pv)	nml_dv_resize(&pv, n)
complex	pv = nml_dcv_new(n)	nml_dcv_delete(pv)	nml_dcv_resize(&pv, n)

matrix	pseudo-constructor	destructor		reconstructor
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
integer	pM = nml_im_new(n)	nml_im_delete(pM)	nml_im_resize(&pM, n)
real	pM = nml_dm_new(n)	nml_dm_delete(pM)	nml_dm_resize(&pM, n)
complex	pM = nml_dcm_new(n)	nml_dcm_delete(pM)	nml_dcm_resize(&pM, n)

to create, destroy or recreate vector and matrix objects.
Notice that the reconstructor must be passed
a pointer to a pointer to a vector or matrix object
so that it can change the pointer itself.
It returns a pointer to the resized vector or matrix object as well.

A sequence of type definitions:

	typedef double		nml_double;
	typedef nml_double	nml_dscalar;
	typedef struct {
	  /* public: */
	  nml_double	r, i;
	  } nml_dcomplex;
	typedef nml_dcomplex	nml_dcscalar;

defines [Cartesian] complex numbers.
There are functions:

	nml_double r = nml_dcreal(c);	/*      real part of c	*/
	nml_double i = nml_dcimag(c);	/* imaginary part of c	*/

to retrieve the real or imaginary part of a complex number
and a pseudo-constructor

	nml_dcomplex c = nml_dcmplx(r, i);

to create a complex number from two real numbers.

